const beforeForm = "<blockquote class=\"bg-teal-50 border-l-4 border-[#00A99D] rounded-r-xl p-5 mb-10\">\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>TL;DR</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Winter causes different basement problems than spring: frozen pipes and sump lines, snowmelt on frozen ground, power outages, a sealed-up house, and mice looking for warmth.</li>\n<li>Before the first hard freeze: drain outdoor faucets, insulate exposed pipes, seal the rim joist, and fix the sump discharge line so it can&#39;t freeze shut.</li>\n<li>Before the first snow: clear gutters after the leaves drop, extend downspouts, cover window wells, test the sump pump and its backup, and decide where shoveled snow will go.</li>\n<li>Through the winter: keep furnace and water heater vents clear of snow, run the dehumidifier differently (or not at all), test for radon, and check the basement after every big storm and thaw.</li>\n<li>A foot of snow on a 2,000 square foot roof melts into roughly 1,250 gallons of water. Where that water goes in March is decided by what you do in October.</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Spring maintenance is about rain. Fall maintenance is about cold, and cold changes how your whole basement behaves. Pipes near the rim joist sit inches from freezing air. Frozen ground can&#39;t absorb meltwater, so it runs toward the foundation instead. A winter storm can knock out the power to your heat and your sump pump at the same moment. And once the windows close for the season, the house starts pulling air, moisture, and radon up out of the ground in ways it doesn&#39;t in summer.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">This guide covers everything that changes in winter, why it matters, and exactly what to do about it. If you already did our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/spring-basement-checklist\">spring basement checklist</a>, you won&#39;t repeat much of it here. That list handles rain-season maintenance like the dryer vent, water heater flush, and condensate line. This one handles cold.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Plan on one weekend in October or early November for the main work, then a few minutes after big storms through the winter.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Why Winter Is Hard on Basements</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Four things happen once temperatures drop, and almost every item on this checklist traces back to one of them.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>1. Water freezes where you can&#39;t see it.</strong> The rim joist, outdoor faucet lines, and the sump discharge pipe all sit at the boundary between your heated basement and the outdoors. They&#39;re the first things to freeze, and they&#39;re usually in spots you don&#39;t look at until something breaks.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>2. Frozen ground turns snowmelt into runoff.</strong> In summer, rain soaks into the soil. In late winter, the top layer of soil is frozen solid while the snow on top melts. The water has nowhere to go but sideways, and the low spot it finds is often your foundation, window wells, or the floor drain. This is why so many basements leak during a February or March thaw, not during the storm itself.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>3. The stack effect kicks in.</strong> When it&#39;s cold outside, warm air rises through your house and leaks out the upper floors. That pulls replacement air in at the lowest level, through the rim joist, cracks in the foundation, and gaps around pipes. It&#39;s why basements feel drafty in winter, why radon levels often climb, and why cold spots near the rim joist freeze pipes.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>4. You close the house up.</strong> Windows stay shut for months. Moisture from cooking, showers, and breathing stays inside. Furnaces and water heaters run constantly. That changes your humidity, your air quality, and your carbon monoxide risk.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">The First-Frost Rule</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">You don&#39;t have to do everything in one weekend. Sort the work by deadline, and get the top row done first:</p>\n<div class=\"w-full max-w-full overflow-x-auto mb-6\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable checklist or comparison table\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"w-full min-w-[640px] text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200 rounded-lg\">\n<thead class=\"bg-[#1B2A3B] text-white\">\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Deadline</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Jobs</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Why this deadline</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>Before the first hard freeze</strong> (several hours below about 25°F)</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Drain outdoor faucets, insulate exposed pipes, seal the rim joist, fix the sump discharge line, find the main water shutoff</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">These cause the most expensive damage, and they can&#39;t be fixed easily once the ground and pipes freeze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>Before the first real snow</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Clean gutters, extend downspouts, cover window wells, test the sump pump and backup, plan snow piles, buy a roof rake if you get ice dams</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Snow buries these jobs or makes them dangerous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>Early winter, then ongoing</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Adjust the dehumidifier, test for radon, check CO alarms and furnace vents, organize storage, watch for pests, monthly checks</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">These depend on the house being closed up and the heat running</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">If you live where winters are mild and hard freezes are rare, you can skip most of the freeze protection in the first row, but snowmelt, outages, humidity, and CO still apply.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">5-Minute Walk-Through: What Does Your Basement Need?</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Grab a flashlight and a notepad. Walk the basement first, then the outside of the house.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>Inside:</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Exposed pipes:</strong> Do any run along an exterior wall or across the top of the foundation? Put your hand near them. Do you feel a draft?</li>\n<li><strong>Outdoor faucet lines:</strong> Follow each one from where it exits the wall. Is there a shutoff valve on it inside?</li>\n<li><strong>Main water shutoff:</strong> Can you find it in under a minute? Does it turn?</li>\n<li><strong>Sump pump:</strong> Is the pit clean? When did you last test it? Is there a backup?</li>\n<li><strong>Furnace and water heater:</strong> Is the area around them clear? Is there a CO alarm on this level?</li>\n<li><strong>Humidity:</strong> What does your hygrometer say? (If you don&#39;t own one, put that at the top of the list.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>Outside:</strong>\n7. <strong>Faucets:</strong> Are hoses still connected?\n8. <strong>Gutters and downspouts:</strong> Are gutters full? Do downspouts empty within a few feet of the foundation?\n9. <strong>Window wells:</strong> Full of leaves? Covered?\n10. <strong>Sump discharge:</strong> Where does it come out? Is the end buried, kinked, or sitting in a low spot?\n11. <strong>Furnace and water heater vents:</strong> If you have white PVC pipes coming out the side of the house, how close are they to the ground?</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Most basements turn up three to six real jobs. The sections below explain each one.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Outside the House</h2>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Shut Off and Drain Outdoor Faucets</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Frozen outdoor faucet lines are among the most common winter plumbing failures, and the break usually happens inside the wall or the basement ceiling, where you won&#39;t see it until the water is already spreading.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>How to do it:</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Disconnect every hose, splitter, and timer from the outdoor faucets. A connected hose traps water in the faucet even if the faucet is frost-free.</li>\n<li>Inside the basement, find the shutoff valve on the line that feeds each outdoor faucet and close it.</li>\n<li>Go back outside and open the outdoor faucet. Water will drain out of the line.</li>\n<li>Back inside, look for a small cap on the side of the shutoff valve. That&#39;s a bleeder cap. Put a cup under it and loosen it to let the last of the water out of the pipe, then tighten it again.</li>\n<li>Leave the outdoor faucet open all winter.</li>\n<li>Add an insulated <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B0hX3hBmk\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">faucet cover</a> outside for extra protection.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>How to tell if you have a frost-free faucet:</strong> a frost-free faucet has a long body that extends through the wall, so the actual valve sits inside where it&#39;s warm. If the handle is on a long stem and the faucet tilts slightly downward toward the outside, it&#39;s likely frost-free. Even these freeze and split if a hose is left on.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>No interior shutoff?</strong> That faucet is exposed all winter. A plumber can add a shutoff or replace it with a frost-free model. Until then, the faucet cover is your main protection. For more on pipes that freeze, see our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/frozen-pipes-in-basement\">frozen pipes guide</a>.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Clean Gutters After the Leaves Drop</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Clean them too early and you&#39;ll be back on the ladder in three weeks. Wait until most of the trees around the house are bare, then clear them out and flush each downspout with a hose to make sure it flows.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">This matters for the basement because clogged gutters cause ice dams. Snow on a warm roof melts, runs down to the cold gutter, and refreezes. The ice builds into a dam, and meltwater backs up behind it. Some leaks into the walls. The rest pours over the edge and lands right against the foundation.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">If your house gets ice dams every year, a roof rake lets you pull the bottom few feet of snow off the roof from the ground after big storms. That&#39;s usually enough to stop dams from forming. Repeated ice dams also point to an attic insulation or ventilation problem worth having looked at.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Extend Downspouts and Pick a Snow Pile Spot</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Downspouts should release water at least 6 feet from the foundation. In winter, that distance matters even more, because the ground near the house is frozen and can&#39;t absorb anything.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>Choosing an extension for winter:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Flexible or hinged extensions</strong> like a <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B03aFm4eB\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">flexible downspout extension</a> are easy to add and can be flipped up out of the way for snow removal. Check that they don&#39;t sag into a low spot where water pools and freezes.</li>\n<li><strong>Rigid extensions</strong> hold their slope better in snow and ice.</li>\n<li><strong>Buried downspout lines</strong> move water farthest, but they can freeze solid in winter. If yours ice up, the water backs up and overflows at the foundation. Check where the line comes out, keep the outlet clear of snow, and consider a surface extension for the winter months if the buried line freezes every year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>Decide where shoveled snow will go now, before the first storm.</strong> Piling snow against the foundation, on top of window wells, over the sump discharge outlet, or on the downhill side of the driveway toward the house is one of the most common ways people flood their own basement in a thaw. Pick a spot that&#39;s downhill and away from the house, and tell everyone who shovels.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">To put the scale in perspective: snow typically melts down to about a tenth of its depth in water. One foot of snow over a 2,000 square foot roof is roughly 1,250 gallons, and nearly all of it leaves through your downspouts. A snow pile the size of a car holds a lot more.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Clear and Cover Window Wells</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Window wells are low spots by design, which makes them a natural collection point for snowmelt.</p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Clear out leaves and debris.</strong> Most wells have a gravel bottom or a small drain that leads to the footing drain. Leaves block it.</li>\n<li><strong>Pour a bucket of water into the well.</strong> It should drain in a few minutes. If it sits, the drain is clogged or the gravel needs to be replaced.</li>\n<li><strong>Add a cover.</strong> A cover keeps snow and ice out of the well entirely. Sloped or dome covers shed snow best, and heavy snow areas need a cover with a published weight rating. See our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/best-window-well-covers-egress-ladders\">window well cover guide</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep egress windows usable.</strong> If a window is an emergency exit, the cover has to open easily from inside, and snow can&#39;t be piled on top of it. Check this after every storm.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Fix the Sump Pump Discharge Line</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">The pipe that carries water from the sump pump to the outside is the part of the system most likely to freeze, and a frozen discharge line can burn out a working pump. When the outlet freezes, the pump keeps running against a blocked pipe, overheats, and fails, often in the middle of a thaw when you need it most.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>How to set it up for winter:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Make sure the line slopes away from the house</strong> all the way to the outlet, so no water sits in it between pump cycles.</li>\n<li><strong>Ditch flexible corrugated hose for winter.</strong> Ridged hose lying on the ground holds water in every ridge, and it freezes fast. A rigid pipe with a steady slope drains much better.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the outlet clear.</strong> It shouldn&#39;t be buried under mulch, snow, or a snow pile. Mark it with a stake so you can find it after a storm.</li>\n<li><strong>Add a freeze guard fitting.</strong> A sump discharge freeze guard installs on the line just outside the house. If the pipe farther out freezes, water escapes through an opening in the fitting instead of backing up to the pump. It&#39;s an inexpensive fitting that protects an expensive pump.</li>\n<li><strong>If you have a buried discharge line,</strong> it&#39;s the most likely to freeze. Ask a plumber whether it&#39;s deep enough and sloped correctly, or whether it needs an overflow outlet.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Keep Furnace and Water Heater Vents Clear</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">If your furnace or water heater is a high-efficiency model, it probably vents through two white PVC pipes on the side of the house instead of a chimney. One brings in air, the other sends out exhaust. If snow or ice blocks either one, the appliance shuts itself off, which means no heat in the middle of a storm. A blocked exhaust can also push combustion gases back toward the house.</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Find the vent pipes now, before they&#39;re buried. Note how high they sit above the ground.</li>\n<li>After every snowstorm, clear snow away from them, including drifts and snow thrown by a snowblower.</li>\n<li>Don&#39;t stack firewood, bins, or anything else in front of them.</li>\n<li>If they sit close to the ground in a spot that drifts, ask an HVAC tech about raising them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Do the same for the dryer vent. A vent buried in snow traps lint and moisture and can push exhaust back inside.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Inside: Pipes and Water</h2>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Insulate Exposed Pipes</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Any pipe within a few feet of an exterior wall or running along the top of the foundation is at risk once temperatures drop into the teens. <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B03ZGr3vl\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Foam pipe insulation</a> costs a few dollars per section and goes on in minutes: measure the pipe, slip on the sleeve, and tape the seams. Cover elbows and valves too, since they freeze first.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Pipes that have frozen before need more than insulation. Our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/frozen-pipes-in-basement\">frozen pipes guide</a> covers heat cable, which pipes need what, and what to do if one freezes.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Seal the Rim Joist</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">The rim joist is the band of wood framing that sits on top of the foundation wall, and in most basements it&#39;s the single leakiest spot. It&#39;s where cold air comes in, where pipes freeze, where the floor above gets its chill, and where mice find their way inside.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>How to find the leaks:</strong> on a cold, windy day, hold a lit incense stick or a strip of tissue near the top of the foundation wall and move slowly along it. Where the smoke blows sideways or the tissue flutters, air is coming in. Pay extra attention to spots where pipes, wires, and vents pass through.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>What to seal with:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Small gaps and cracks:</strong> canned spray foam like <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B0dWe2ero\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Great Stuff Gaps &amp; Cracks</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gaps around pipes and wires:</strong> spray foam, or caulk for very small gaps.</li>\n<li><strong>The whole rim joist cavity:</strong> rigid foam board cut to fit and sealed around the edges, or professional spray foam. Our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/basement-insulation-guide\">basement insulation guide</a> and <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/spray-foam-vs-rigid-foam-insulation\">spray foam vs. rigid foam guide</a> cover full insulation jobs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>One caution:</strong> if your furnace or water heater burns gas or oil and draws its air from the basement, don&#39;t seal the space so tightly that it can&#39;t breathe. See the section on combustion air below.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Find, Test, and Tag the Main Water Shutoff</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">If a pipe bursts in January, every minute you spend looking for the shutoff is more water on the floor.</p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Find it.</strong> It&#39;s usually on the foundation wall where the water line comes into the house, often near the water meter or, on a well, near the pressure tank.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify the type.</strong> A lever handle (ball valve) shuts off with a quarter turn. A round wheel handle (gate valve) takes several turns and is more likely to stick or fail in older homes.</li>\n<li><strong>Test it.</strong> Turn it off, open a faucet to confirm the water stops, then turn it back on. If it&#39;s stuck, don&#39;t force it. Have a plumber replace it now, while it&#39;s a scheduled job instead of an emergency.</li>\n<li><strong>Tag it.</strong> Hang a bright tag on it so anyone in the house, or a neighbor checking in while you&#39;re away, can find it fast.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">While you&#39;re there, find the water heater&#39;s cold water shutoff too.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Add a Temperature and Humidity Alert</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">A Wi-Fi sensor near your most exposed pipe alerts your phone if the basement gets too cold, which is the warning you need before a pipe freezes. The <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B02NAJAHE\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Govee Wi-Fi thermometer</a> also tracks humidity, so it does double duty for the dehumidifier section below. Set the low-temperature alert around 40°F. For water leak alerts, see our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/best-water-leak-detectors\">best water leak detectors</a>.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Inside: Doors, Windows, and Crawl Spaces</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Basement windows and doors are often the least efficient in the house. Older single-pane basement windows lose heat fast and collect condensation and frost on the inside.</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Check weatherstripping</strong> on basement doors, especially a walkout or bulkhead door. If you can see daylight around the edges, replace it. A door sweep closes the gap at the bottom.</li>\n<li><strong>Seal old basement windows</strong> with a window insulation film kit. The clear plastic film tapes around the frame and shrinks tight with a hair dryer. It&#39;s nearly invisible and cuts drafts and condensation for a season. Skip it on any window that serves as an emergency exit.</li>\n<li><strong>Caulk gaps</strong> around window frames where they meet the foundation.</li>\n<li><strong>If part of your basement opens to a vented crawl space,</strong> close the crawl space vents for winter so cold air doesn&#39;t flow under the house and freeze pipes there, unless the crawl space is encapsulated and designed to stay sealed year-round. See our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/crawl-space-encapsulation-guide\">crawl space encapsulation guide</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Sump Pump and Power Outages</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Winter storms knock out power, and the thaw afterward can push water toward the house while the ground is still frozen. Find problems with the sump pump now, not in March.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Test the Main Pump</h3>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Clear any debris from the pit. Gravel, silt, or a fallen object can jam the float.</li>\n<li>Slowly pour a bucket or two of water into the pit until the float rises.</li>\n<li>The pump should start, empty the pit, and shut off. Listen for a thud when it stops. That&#39;s the check valve closing, and it keeps water in the pipe from draining back into the pit.</li>\n<li>Go outside while someone runs the test again, and confirm water comes out of the discharge outlet.</li>\n<li>If the pump hums but doesn&#39;t move water, runs and won&#39;t stop, or the float sticks, see our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/sump-pump-buying-guide\">sump pump buying guide</a> and <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/cost-guides/sump-pump-replacement-cost\">sump pump replacement cost guide</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Test the Backup</h3>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Battery backup:</strong> press the test button if it has one, or unplug the main pump and pour water in to trigger the backup. Check the battery&#39;s age. Most need replacing every few years, and a weak battery is the most common reason backups fail.</li>\n<li><strong>Water-powered backup:</strong> it runs on city water pressure. Trigger it by raising its float and confirm it pumps. It won&#39;t work if your home is on a well and the power is out.</li>\n<li><strong>No backup at all?</strong> Fall is the time to add one. See our guides to <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/backup-power-sump-pumps\">backup power for sump pumps</a> and <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/battery-vs-water-powered-sump-pumps\">battery vs. water-powered backups</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Get Ready for an Outage</h3>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Keep a flashlight, extra batteries, and a wet/dry vacuum in the basement where you can find them in the dark.</li>\n<li>Know which circuit your sump pump is on.</li>\n<li>If you own a generator, run it once before winter and confirm it can power the sump pump.</li>\n<li>Review our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/basement-emergency-water-plan\">basement emergency water plan</a> so everyone knows what to do if water starts coming in.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Air, Humidity, and Safety</h2>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Change How You Run the Dehumidifier</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Winter air holds much less moisture, so most basements need far less dehumidifying from November through March. There&#39;s also a mechanical issue. Standard compressor dehumidifiers work by cooling air over cold coils, and once the basement drops below about 65°F, those coils start to frost over. Many models have an auto-defrost mode, but they spend more time defrosting than drying.</p>\n<div class=\"w-full max-w-full overflow-x-auto mb-6\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable checklist or comparison table\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"w-full min-w-[640px] text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200 rounded-lg\">\n<thead class=\"bg-[#1B2A3B] text-white\">\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Your basement in winter</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">What to do with the dehumidifier</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Heated and finished, above 65°F</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Keep it running. Set it to 45 to 50%. It will run much less than in summer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Cool, 55°F to 65°F</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Run it only if humidity stays above 50%. Expect it to defrost often.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Cold and unfinished, below 55°F</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">If humidity stays under 50% on its own, clean it and store it until spring.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Cold but humidity stays above 50%</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">You likely have a water source to fix. A low-temperature-rated model can help in the meantime.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>Two other winter checks:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>If the dehumidifier drains through a hose,</strong> make sure the hose doesn&#39;t run outside or through an unheated space where it can freeze and back up.</li>\n<li><strong>Before storing it,</strong> empty and dry the bucket, clean the filter, and let the unit dry out for a day so it doesn&#39;t grow mold over the winter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>Don&#39;t overcorrect.</strong> Winter air in a heated house can get very dry. Below about 30% humidity, you may notice static, dry skin, and gaps opening in wood floors. Somewhere between 30 and 50% is the target. See our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/basement-humidity-guide\">basement humidity guide</a> and <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/best-basement-dehumidifiers\">best basement dehumidifiers</a>.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Watch for Winter Condensation</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Foundation walls get very cold in winter. When warm, moist air from the house reaches them, the moisture condenses into water, the same way a cold glass sweats in summer. It shows up most behind furniture, stored boxes, and finished walls, where air doesn&#39;t circulate.</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Pull boxes, shelves, and furniture a few inches away from exterior walls.</li>\n<li>Don&#39;t stack storage directly against foundation walls.</li>\n<li>Keep an eye on the corners and behind the water heater.</li>\n<li>Insulating the walls is the long-term fix, since a warmer wall surface doesn&#39;t condense.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">If you see water on the walls and aren&#39;t sure whether it&#39;s condensation or a leak, our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/wet-basement-walls\">wet basement walls guide</a> walks through how to tell the difference.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Test for Radon in Winter</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Radon is a radioactive gas that seeps up from the soil, and it&#39;s the second leading cause of lung cancer in the U.S. Winter is a good time to test, because the stack effect pulls more soil gas into the basement and the house is closed up anyway.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>How to run a winter test:</strong></p>\n<ol class=\"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Use a short-term test kit and place it in the lowest level you use regularly, at about breathing height, away from drafts, exterior walls, and the sump pit.</li>\n<li>Keep windows and exterior doors closed for at least 12 hours before the test starts and throughout it, except for normal coming and going.</li>\n<li>Leave it for the full time the kit specifies, then mail it to the lab.</li>\n<li>If the result is 4 pCi/L or higher, the EPA recommends fixing it. Levels between 2 and 4 are worth considering too.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/radon-testing-and-mitigation\">radon testing and mitigation guide</a> covers kits, results, and mitigation systems.</p>\n<h3 class=\"text-xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-3 mt-8\">Furnace, Combustion Air, and Carbon Monoxide</h3>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">The furnace and water heater run harder in winter than at any other time of year. If either one burns gas, oil, or propane and sits in the basement, three things need attention.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>1. A working CO alarm.</strong> Every level with a fuel-burning appliance needs one. A plug-in alarm with a battery backup like the <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B07tcNN1S\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">First Alert CO605</a> keeps working during an outage. Replace alarms that are past the date printed on the back.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>2. Combustion air.</strong> Older furnaces and water heaters that vent through a chimney draw the air they burn from the room around them. If you seal the basement too tightly, or close a furnace room door that used to stay open, they can starve for air. That can cause backdrafting, where exhaust spills back into the basement instead of going up the chimney. Never block louvered doors or vents on a furnace room, and if you&#39;ve done a lot of air sealing, ask an HVAC tech to check for backdrafting.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>3. Clearance and service.</strong> Clear stored boxes, paint, and cleaning products away from the furnace and water heater. Most manufacturers call for at least a few feet of open space, and flammable items stacked against a burner are a fire risk. Change the furnace filter before heating season starts, and schedule a tune-up if it hasn&#39;t been serviced in the last year.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Heat and Comfort</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">An unheated basement in a cold climate can drop low enough to freeze pipes during a long cold snap, especially near the rim joist.</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Keep the basement above about 50°F</strong> if it has water pipes, and the whole house at 55°F or higher when you&#39;re away.</li>\n<li><strong>If the basement has no heat source,</strong> a heater with an anti-freeze setting, like the <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"https://link.amazon/B03wKYCHp\" rel=\"sponsored nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">De&#39;Longhi oil-filled radiator</a>, switches on automatically near 41°F.</li>\n<li><strong>If you use the basement in winter,</strong> plan the heat before the first cold snap. Our guide on <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/how-to-heat-a-basement\">how to heat a basement</a> compares space heaters, baseboard heat, ductwork, and mini-splits, with what each costs to run.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Pests</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Mice start looking for warm places as soon as nights drop into the 40s, and they can squeeze through a gap the width of a pencil. The rim joist sealing above blocks the biggest entry point. Pay attention to gaps around the dryer vent, the gas line, cable and phone lines, and outdoor faucet pipes, and check for droppings along walls and behind stored items in November. For full prevention steps, see our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/basement-mice-pest-prevention\">basement mice and pest prevention guide</a>.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Storage</h2>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Get everything off the floor.</strong> Shelving or pallets keep boxes out of the way if meltwater or a sump failure puts an inch of water on the floor.</li>\n<li><strong>Switch from cardboard to plastic bins with lids.</strong> Cardboard soaks up moisture, grows mold, and gives mice nesting material.</li>\n<li><strong>Leave a few inches between storage and exterior walls</strong> to prevent condensation and mold behind it.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep a clear path</strong> to the main water shutoff, the electrical panel, the sump pump, and the furnace.</li>\n<li><strong>In an unheated basement,</strong> move anything that can be ruined by freezing, like latex paint, some cleaning products, and canned goods, to a warmer spot.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">If You&#39;ll Be Away This Winter</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">A basement problem in an empty house can run for days before anyone notices.</p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Keep the thermostat at 55°F or higher.</li>\n<li>Leave the Wi-Fi temperature sensor and leak detectors running, and make sure someone can get to the house if they alert.</li>\n<li>Ask a neighbor or friend to check the basement after big storms and thaws, and show them where the main water shutoff is.</li>\n<li>For a long absence, consider shutting off the main water and draining the lines. Don&#39;t do this if your home has a boiler or radiant heat that runs on water, or a fire sprinkler system. Ask a plumber first.</li>\n<li>Confirm the sump pump and backup are working before you leave.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Monthly Checks Through the Winter</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">The fall work does most of the job. These few minutes a month catch problems early.</p>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>Once a month, December through March:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Check the hygrometer and adjust the dehumidifier if needed.</li>\n<li>Look at the sump pit. Is the water level normal? Is the pump cycling?</li>\n<li>Walk the exterior walls and look for condensation, damp spots, or frost.</li>\n<li>Look for mouse droppings along walls and behind storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>After every big snowstorm:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Clear snow away from furnace and water heater vents, the dryer vent, window wells, and the sump discharge outlet.</li>\n<li>Pull snow off the lower part of the roof if you get ice dams.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>During every thaw:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>Check the basement within a day. Thaws, not storms, cause most winter leaks.</li>\n<li>Watch the sump pump and confirm water is leaving the outlet.</li>\n<li>Look at window wells and the base of the walls for water.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Your Winter Calendar</h2>\n<div class=\"w-full max-w-full overflow-x-auto mb-6\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable checklist or comparison table\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"w-full min-w-[640px] text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200 rounded-lg\">\n<thead class=\"bg-[#1B2A3B] text-white\">\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">When</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>Late September to October</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Clean up the yard, plan snow pile spots, buy supplies, schedule a furnace tune-up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>October to early November, before the first hard freeze</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Drain outdoor faucets, insulate pipes, seal the rim joist, fix the sump discharge, find the main shutoff</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>November, once the leaves are down</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Clean gutters, extend downspouts, cover window wells, test the sump pump and backup, add window film and door sweeps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>Early December</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Adjust or store the dehumidifier, start a radon test, check CO alarms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>December through February</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Monthly checks, clear vents and window wells after storms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>Late February to March, the thaw</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Check the basement after every warm spell, watch the sump pump, keep snow piles away from the house</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\"><strong>April</strong></td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Switch to the <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/spring-basement-checklist\">spring basement checklist</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">The Complete Fall Basement Checklist</h2>\n<div class=\"w-full max-w-full overflow-x-auto mb-6\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable checklist or comparison table\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"w-full min-w-[640px] text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200 rounded-lg\">\n<thead class=\"bg-[#1B2A3B] text-white\">\n<tr>\n<th class=\"w-16 p-3 text-center font-semibold\">Done</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Task</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Deadline</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Disconnect hoses, shut off and drain outdoor faucets, open bleeder caps</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first freeze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Insulate exposed pipes near exterior walls and the rim joist</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first freeze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Find and seal rim joist air leaks</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first freeze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Set up the sump discharge line for winter, add a freeze guard</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first freeze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Find, test, and tag the main water shutoff</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first freeze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Clean gutters after the leaves drop</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first snow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Extend downspouts at least 6 feet from the foundation</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first snow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Clear, test, and cover window wells</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first snow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Test the sump pump and its backup</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first snow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Pick a snow pile spot away from the house</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first snow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Locate furnace and water heater vents outside</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first snow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Weatherstrip basement doors, add window film to old windows</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before first snow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Add a temperature and humidity sensor</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Early winter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Adjust or store the dehumidifier</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Early winter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Run a radon test</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Early winter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Check CO alarms, clear space around the furnace, change the filter</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Early winter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Move storage off the floor and away from walls</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Early winter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Check for signs of mice</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Early winter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"w-16 p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700 text-center align-middle whitespace-nowrap text-xl leading-none\" style=\"font-family:Arial,Segoe UI Symbol,sans-serif\">☐</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Make a plan for while you&#39;re away</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Before you travel</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Cost Breakdown and 10-Year Total Cost of Ownership</h2>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Most of this checklist is free. Here&#39;s what the paid parts typically cost, compared with what they prevent.</p>\n<div class=\"w-full max-w-full overflow-x-auto mb-6\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable checklist or comparison table\" tabindex=\"0\"><table class=\"w-full min-w-[640px] text-sm border-collapse border border-gray-200 rounded-lg\">\n<thead class=\"bg-[#1B2A3B] text-white\">\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Item</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Typical cost</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">Lasts</th>\n<th class=\"text-left p-3 font-semibold\">10-year cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Foam pipe insulation</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$15 to $50</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Many years</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$15 to $50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Spray foam for rim joist gaps</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$10 to $40</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Many years</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$10 to $40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Outdoor faucet covers</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$5 to $15 each</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Several winters</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$15 to $45 each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Downspout extensions</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$10 to $30 each</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Several years</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$20 to $90 each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Sump discharge freeze guard</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Usually under $30</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Many years</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Under $30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Roof rake</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$40 to $100</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Many years</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$40 to $100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Window insulation film</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$15 to $30 per kit</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">One season</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$150 to $300 if used yearly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Door sweep and weatherstripping</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$10 to $40</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Several years</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$20 to $80</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Temperature and humidity sensor</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">About $35</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Several years</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$35 to $70</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Radon test kit</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">About $15 to $30</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">One test</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$30 to $150, testing every few years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Battery backup sump pump</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Several hundred dollars installed</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Batteries every few years</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">See our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/backup-power-sump-pumps\">backup power guide</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Furnace tune-up</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">Typically $100 to $200</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">One year</td>\n<td class=\"p-3 border-b border-gray-200 text-gray-700\">$1,000 to $2,000 if done yearly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\"><strong>What they prevent:</strong></p>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li>A burst pipe often means $400 to $1,500 in plumbing repair, plus water damage cleanup that commonly runs into the thousands.</li>\n<li>A burned-out sump pump has to be replaced in an emergency, usually during the thaw when the basement is already taking on water. See our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/cost-guides/sump-pump-replacement-cost\">sump pump replacement cost guide</a>.</li>\n<li>A flooded finished basement can mean new drywall, flooring, and mold remediation. See our <a class=\"text-[#00A99D] underline\" href=\"/articles/mold-remediation-cost\">mold remediation cost guide</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Everything on the list above, done every year for a decade, typically costs less than one of those events.</p>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">Common Mistakes</h2>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Leaving a hose connected.</strong> It traps water in the faucet, even a frost-free one, and splits it.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting the bleeder cap.</strong> Closing the inside valve without draining the pipe leaves water in it to freeze.</li>\n<li><strong>Cleaning gutters before the leaves are down.</strong> You&#39;ll do it twice.</li>\n<li><strong>Shoveling snow against the foundation or into window wells.</strong> It all melts at once during the first warm spell.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaving a corrugated sump discharge hose on the ground.</strong> It freezes, and the pump burns out trying to push through it.</li>\n<li><strong>Letting snow bury furnace vents.</strong> The furnace shuts down, usually at night during the storm.</li>\n<li><strong>Sealing a furnace room airtight.</strong> Older fuel-burning appliances need air to burn safely.</li>\n<li><strong>Running a standard dehumidifier in a cold basement.</strong> It frosts up, runs constantly, and removes almost no water.</li>\n<li><strong>Stacking storage against exterior walls.</strong> Condensation and mold form behind it.</li>\n<li><strong>Only checking the basement after storms.</strong> Thaws cause more winter leaks than snowfall.</li>\n<li><strong>Testing radon with windows open.</strong> Short-term tests need closed-house conditions to mean anything.</li>\n<li><strong>Turning the heat way down when traveling.</strong> Keep the house at 55°F or higher.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 class=\"text-2xl font-bold text-[#1B2A3B] mb-4 mt-10\">When to Call a Pro</h2>\n<ul class=\"list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6\">\n<li><strong>Plumber:</strong> an outdoor faucet has no interior shutoff, the main water shutoff is stuck or leaking, or you want the system drained for a long absence</li>\n<li><strong>Plumber or waterproofing contractor:</strong> the sump pump fails its test, you want a backup installed, or the buried discharge line freezes every year</li>\n<li><strong>HVAC tech:</strong> the furnace hasn&#39;t been serviced in more than a year, vents sit too close to the ground, or you&#39;re worried about combustion air after air sealing</li>\n<li><strong>Radon mitigation contractor:</strong> your test comes back at 4 pCi/L or higher</li>\n<li><strong>Waterproofing contractor:</strong> you see water coming through walls or the floor, not just condensation</li>\n<li><strong>Roofer or insulation contractor:</strong> you get ice dams every winter</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed\">Most everything else on this list is DIY and fits in a weekend.</p>\n"
const glossary = "<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Hard freeze:</dt><dd>Several hours below about 25°F, cold enough to freeze exposed pipes and plants.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Rim joist:</dt><dd>The wood framing that sits on top of the foundation wall. The leakiest spot in most basements.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Bleeder cap:</dt><dd>A small cap on a shutoff valve that lets you drain the water left in the pipe after the valve is closed.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Frost-free faucet:</dt><dd>An outdoor faucet with its valve inside the warm wall so it doesn't freeze.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Stack effect:</dt><dd>Warm air rising and leaking out of the upper house, which pulls cold air and soil gas in at the basement level.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Ice dam:</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps meltwater, which then leaks into walls or spills against the foundation.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Sump discharge line:</dt><dd>The pipe that carries water from the sump pump to the outside.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Combustion air:</dt><dd>The air a fuel-burning furnace or water heater draws in to burn fuel safely.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">Backdrafting:</dt><dd>When exhaust from a fuel-burning appliance spills back into the house instead of venting outside.</dd></div>\n<div><dt class=\"font-bold text-[#1B2A3B]\">pCi/L:</dt><dd>Picocuries per liter, the unit used to measure radon.</dd></div>"
